  "raw": " replaced by John Hampden. Burke, who was returning under a new Conservative banner, had defeated Sir John Hutchinson and he was re-elected at the 1795 general election on the Conservative ticket, holding the seat until he died in December 1797. In his younger days, he had been in favour of the monarchy and while he did not support any particular government he did have strong thoughts in general terms on the issue of the king as head of state. Burke was well regarded in Parliament and was a member of the House of Commons from 1774 to 1795, at its dissolution he was returned as M.P. for the city and parliamentary county of Leicestershire. By 1798 he was known as a man of influence within the Conservative camp, Burke first became aware of the French Revolution through the study of the work of Thomas Jefferson, whom he found particularly interesting as a philosopher. A few weeks later he watched the news of the Revolution being broadcast in a newspaper from London to Dublin and",
